,{Z}_{p}\\subseteq V(G) for p=\\lceil \\sqrt{k}\\rceil . Suppose S\\subseteq V(G) is an unknown solution for (\\mathrm{\\Gamma },w,\\delta ,k). --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNote that this definition is different from graph theoretic edge deletion as all the half-edges of the graph remain and the corollas are unchanged. We neither lose vertices nor half-edges when removing an internal edge. We just unglue the two corollas connected by that edge, or to put it another way, the edge is split into two external edges by separating the two half edges forming it. (https://www2.mathematik.hu-berlin.de/~kreimer/wp-content/uploads/june22.pdf)\nciteturn5search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.4 years ago; Crawled: 3 days ago; Note that this definition is different from graph theoretic edge deletion as all the half-edges of the graph remain and the corollas are unchanged. ... The graph resulting from the contraction of edge \\(e\\), denoted \\(\\Gamma/e\\) for \\(e\\in E_\\Gamma\\), is defined to be\nNote that this definition is different from graph theoretic edge deletion as all the half-edges of the graph remain and the corollas are unchanged. We neither lose vertices nor half-edges when removing an internal edge. We just unglue the two corollas connected by that edge, or to put it another way, the edge is split into two external edges by separating the two half edges forming it.\n\nThe graph resulting from the contraction of edge \\(e\\), denoted \\(\\Gamma/e\\) for \\(e\\in E_\\Gamma\\), is defined to be\n\n(A.1) \\(\\quad \\Gamma/e=(H_\\Gamma-e,\\mathcal V'_\\Gamma,E_\\Gamma-e)\\)\n\nwhere \\(\\mathcal V'_\\Gamma\\) is the partition which is the same as \\(\\mathcal V_\\Gamma\\) except that in place of the parts \\(c_v\\) and \\(c_w\\) for \\(e=\\{v,w\\}\\), \\(\\mathcal V'\\) has a single part \\((c_v\\cup c_w)-e\\).¹⁴ Likewise we define \\(\\Gamma/X\\), for \\(X\\subseteq\\Gamma\\) a (not necessarily connected) graph, t